findings.detail:
The new aggregate reads the whole open set into the Node process rather than pushing the sums into the database:

  // No `take` — the OPEN set is read whole, not capped like a rendering page.
  this.prisma.forTenant().invoice.findMany({
    where: { status: { notIn: KPI_SUMMARY_EXCLUDED }, ...(customerId ? { customerId } : {}) },
    select: { total: true, dueDate: true, payments: { select: { amount: true, status: true } } },
  })

and then loops it in JS (lines ~3040-3060) to build totalOutstanding/overdue/dueToday/dueIn30. Only the avgDays figure is pushed down as a `$queryRaw` AVG.

B12's premise is a tenant whose invoice count exceeds the old `useInvoices({ limit: 999 })` page — i.e. precisely the tenants where this read is largest. Failure scenario: a tenant with 40,000 open (non-PAID/VOID/WRITTEN_OFF/DRAFT) invoices averaging two payment rows each. Every mount of `/invoices` now issues one query materialising ~40,000 invoice rows plus ~80,000 joined payment rows into the API process before a single tile renders; the previous client-side path was hard-bounded at 999 rows. `useInvoiceKpiSummary` (apps/web/lib/api/invoices.ts:246-252) declares no `staleTime` — the same as the `useInvoices` hook it replaces, so that part is parity, not a regression — but React Query's default staleTime of 0 means the query re-runs on every mount and window refocus of the invoices page. The tile values themselves are correct; the risk is latency and API memory on the large tenants, and there is no `take`, no cursor, and no server-side aggregate to bound it.

I have not measured this — it is a read of the query shape, not a benchmark — so treat it as a scale concern to rule on rather than an observed failure.

